  "hero.kicker": "Computing · Systems · a long essay you can play with",
  "hero.title": "Why PCIe Keeps Doubling",
  "hero.sub": "Graphics cards, SSDs and network cards all plug into PCIe. Its speed has doubled six times in over twenty years.<br>What keeps it doubling, and what actually happens on the wire during one read or write?",
  "hero.lead": "We start with a tree: how PCIe connects devices to the CPU, what a lane is made of, and where each generation’s doubling comes from. Then we follow one read or write: how it is cut into packets, what each bit of the header means, how the sender makes sure the other end has room before it sends, how a bad packet is resent, how devices get their addresses at boot, and how a device moves data by itself and then calls the CPU. Last, back to the physics: why faster means shorter traces, why the sixth generation switched to two bits per symbol, and why it then moved to fixed-size blocks. <strong>Every model is a rule from the specification, a table from it, or a textbook result</strong>, scaled down to run in a web page.",
  "hero.note": "An AI wrote this page. Header widths, configuration-space offsets and the MSI address format come from the specification, the Linux kernel headers and the Intel manual. Rates, overheads and latencies were checked item by item against public PCI-SIG material. <strong>Where the text says “tuned parameter”, the value was chosen so the model can compute, not measured</strong>, and the text says how far the conclusion moves when it is halved or doubled. This page covers mechanisms and specifications only, and <strong>does not judge which motherboard or card is worth buying</strong>.",

  "ch1.title": "One tree, not one bus",
  "ch1.law": "PCIe has no shared “bus”. Each device has its own point-to-point link to the device above it, and all the links together form a tree whose root sits in the CPU.",
  "ch1.p1": "Before PCIe, PCI was a real bus: a row of parallel wires with every card hung on it. Only one card could talk at a time, and the more cards, the more crowded it got. Parallel wires also needed the signals on dozens of wires to arrive together, which made higher clock speeds hard. The first PCIe version (2003) changed both. It went <strong>serial</strong>: one pair of wires carries one stream of bits. And it went <strong>point-to-point</strong>: each link joins exactly two ends. Why serial beats parallel is shown in a model you can drag in <a href='../how-sata-works/#ch2'>“Why SATA Has Lasted Over Twenty Years”, Chapter 2</a>.",
  "ch1.p2": "The root of the tree is in the CPU and is called the <strong>root complex</strong>. It connects to memory and CPU cores on one side and has several root ports on the other. Each root port connects to one device, or to a <strong>switch</strong> that fans it out into several more links. The graphics card, the SSD and the network card are the leaves of the tree, called <strong>endpoints</strong>.",
  "ch1.p3": "To the operating system the tree looks just like the old bus. Every PCIe device still appears as a “PCI device”, with the same configuration-space layout, and it talks through a range of memory addresses (Chapter 6). That is how PCIe stays backwards compatible: old drivers do not need to change.",
  "ch1.p4": "Next we walk down the tree: what a lane is and how its speed doubled generation by generation (Chapter 2); how one read or write is cut into packets (Chapter 3); how packets avoid overrunning the receiver (Chapter 4) and how bad ones are resent (Chapter 5); who assigns device addresses at boot (Chapter 6); and how a device moves data by itself and calls the CPU (Chapter 7). Then back to the physics: why faster means shorter traces (Chapter 8), why the sixth generation uses two bits per symbol (Chapter 9), and why it then moved to fixed-size FLITs (Chapter 10).",
  "ch1.limit": "This diagram leaves out the link between the CPU and the chipset, and the links between several CPUs. Some of those are PCIe and some are not. How motherboard lanes are shared among the slots is covered in <a href='../how-a-computer-is-wired/#ch5'>“How the Parts of a Computer Are Wired Together”, Chapter 5</a>.",

  "ch2.title": "One lane, six doublings",
  "ch2.law": "Each generation doubles the speed in only three ways: double the symbols per second; waste less on encoding; or let one symbol carry two bits. From Gen1 to Gen7, all three have been used.",
  "ch2.p1": "A <strong>lane</strong> is two differential pairs, one for each direction (Figure 2). “Differential” means a bit is carried by the voltage difference between two wires. Outside interference hits both wires equally, so the difference stays the same. No clock wire travels alongside the data (the slot does carry a 100 MHz reference clock, but only as a reference for the circuits at each end). The receiver recovers the clock from the transitions in the data, so the data must not stay the same for too long. Making sure it keeps changing is the job of the encoding.",
  "ch2.p2": "Gen1 uses 8b/10b encoding: every 8 bits of data are sent as 10 bits, which guarantees transitions and DC balance, at the cost of {enc10} of the line rate. One lane at 2.5 GT/s (2.5 billion transfers per second) leaves only {g1} GB/s in each direction. Gen2 (2007) doubled the rate to 5 GT/s, giving {g2} GB/s. The details of 8b/10b are the same as in SATA; see <a href='../how-sata-works/#ch3'>“Why SATA Has Lasted Over Twenty Years”, Chapter 3</a>.",
  "ch2.p3": "Gen3 ran into the first real problem. Following the old pattern, the next step would be 10 GT/s. In 2008 PCI-SIG compared two options. One was 8 GT/s with a new encoding, 128b/130b (2 sync bits per 128 bits, with transitions guaranteed by scrambling), giving {o8} Gb/s of usable data. The other was 10 GT/s staying with 8b/10b, giving {o10} Gb/s: almost the same. But the same lane loses {oDb} dB less at 4 GHz (the Nyquist frequency of 8 GT/s) than at 5 GHz, and Chapter 8 shows how much those few dB are worth. So Gen3 (2010) raised the rate only from 5 to 8 GT/s, yet its bandwidth is {g3g2} times Gen2’s, at {g3} GB/s per lane (Figure 3).",
  "ch2.p4": "The next two generations went back to the old pattern: Gen4 at 16 GT/s (2017) and Gen5 at 32 GT/s (2019), with the same encoding and an overhead of {enc130}. Gen5 ×16 gives {g5x16} GB/s in each direction. Beyond that, two voltage levels stop being enough. Gen6 (2022), at 64 GT/s, did not raise the symbol rate. Instead one symbol carries two bits (PAM4, Chapter 9), giving {g6lane} GB/s per lane. The encoding became fixed 256-byte FLITs, with an overhead of {flitOh}, but each packet’s own framing and check bits are dropped as well (Chapter 10). Gen7 (2025) runs at 128 GT/s, about {g7lane} GB/s per lane in each direction.",
  "ch2.p5": "The two ends negotiate when the link comes up. Each reports the speeds and widths it supports, and they settle on the highest they share. So a Gen4 card works in a Gen3 slot, but runs at Gen3; a ×16 card in a slot wired for only 8 lanes runs at ×8. Bandwidth is the product of the two: Gen4 ×8 equals Gen3 ×16, both {g4x8} GB/s. Whether a slower link actually slows a card depends on how much data the card really moves. This page does not run such benchmarks.",
  "ch2.p6": "All the GB/s figures here are per direction, after encoding overhead but before packet headers (Chapter 3). The two directions can run at the same time, and vendors often add them together and call the result “bidirectional”, which doubles the number. For why NVMe SSDs usually use ×4, and where each generation’s limits come from, see <a href='../why-nvme-is-fast/#ch2'>“Why NVMe Is So Fast”, Chapter 2</a>.",
  "ch2.limit": "This does not count the time for link training or for entering and leaving power-saving states. It also leaves out the power-saving mode from Gen6 on, which can switch off some lanes.",
  "ch2.model.title": "The speed the two ends agree on",
