  "ch3.title": "One read or write, several packets",
  "ch3.law": "Everything on PCIe (reads, writes, configuration and interrupts) is the same kind of packet: a transaction-layer packet, or TLP. A write is send-only. A read is a question and an answer: one request goes out, and one or more completion packets carrying data come back.",
  "ch3.p1": "To software, reading or writing a register on a graphics card or SSD is much like reading or writing memory: one instruction, one address. On the link, that instruction becomes a TLP. PCIe has three layers (Figure 4). The <strong>transaction layer</strong> builds the TLP and writes its header: what it is for, the address, the length and who asked. The <strong>data link layer</strong> adds a sequence number and a 32-bit LCRC, used for acknowledgements and replays (Chapter 5). The <strong>physical layer</strong> adds the start and end of the frame and spreads the bytes across the lanes.",
  "ch3.p2": "Each TLP carries 8 extra bytes outside its header: the sequence number, the LCRC and the framing. The header itself is 3 or 4 DW (1 DW = 4 bytes). Addresses below 4 GiB must use the 3-DW short header; above that, the 4-DW header is used.",
  "ch3.p3": "Figure 5 shows every bit of the header. The first fields, Fmt and Type, together say what kind of packet this is. The three bits of Fmt say whether the header is 3 or 4 DW and whether data follows; Type says whether it is memory, I/O, configuration, message or completion. Length has 10 bits, counted in DW, with a maximum of 1024 DW, which is 4096 bytes. The <strong>requester ID</strong> is the sender’s bus:device.function number, and <strong>Tag</strong> is the number it gave this request. A completion copies both back, so the requester can match it up. This is what lets one device have many reads in flight without mixing them up.",
  "ch3.p4": "A packet’s size has two limits. <strong>MPS</strong> (maximum payload size) is the most data one write or completion packet can carry. <strong>MRRS</strong> (maximum read request size) is the most one read request may ask for. Both lie between 128 and 4096 bytes. At boot the system sets MPS to a value that every hop along the path supports; MRRS is the requester’s own setting, chosen for each device by the system or the driver. Read data can also be split into smaller pieces: the specification lets the completer cut completions on 64- or 128-byte boundaries.",
  "ch3.p5": "Playing with the numbers shows how much packet size matters. For a 4 KiB write: at MPS 128 the efficiency is {w128}, at 256 it is {w256} and at 512 it is {w512}; a small 64-byte write manages only {w64}. Reads cost more, because each completion packet carries its own header. 4 KiB as 512-byte requests ({r512q} of them) and 256-byte completions ({r512n} of them) gives {r512}. If the completer splits at 64 bytes, {r64n} completion packets come back and the efficiency drops to {r64}.",
  "ch3.p6": "So the Gen5 ×16 figure of {g5x16} GB/s has to be multiplied by an efficiency first: a large write at MPS 256 keeps {w256}, which is {w256X} GB/s. Read requests travel the other way, and since each direction has its own pair of wires, they do not compete with the writes.",
  "ch3.limit": "This assumes a 4-DW header throughout (64-bit addresses). It leaves out the bandwidth taken by acknowledgements and credit updates (Chapters 4 and 5), and the different arithmetic of Gen6 FLIT mode (Chapter 10).",
  "ch3.model.title": "Cutting one block into packets",
