  "ch4.title": "Count the room before sending",
  "ch4.law": "A PCIe link never drops packets because it is overrun. The receiver tells the sender in advance how much buffer it has (its credits). The sender spends a little with each packet and keeps count. When the credits run out it waits, and the credits come back once the receiver has room again.",
  "ch4.p1": "If the sender simply sent whenever it liked, a full receiver buffer could only drop packets, and those would have to be resent: slow, and hard to keep in order. PCIe’s answer is <strong>credits</strong>. As soon as the link comes up, the receiver reports a header credit and a data credit for each of three classes of packet: posted writes; non-posted requests such as reads, which need a reply; and completions. One data credit is 16 bytes, and one header credit is one packet header.",
  "ch4.p2": "The sender keeps two numbers (Figure 6). CL is the running total of the limit the other end has granted. CC is the running total the sender has spent. To send a packet, it first checks whether CC plus this packet’s cost would exceed CL: if not, it sends; if so, it waits. When the receiver takes a packet out of its buffer, it sends a short UpdateFC packet that raises the limit. The counters are only 8 bits (headers) and 12 bits (data), and they wrap around, so comparisons are done modulo the counter size.",
  "ch4.p3": "The rule is simple; the trouble is the round trip. A packet takes time to cross the link, to be taken out of the buffer and for its UpdateFC to come back. During that time the sender can spend only the credits it has in hand. To keep the modulo comparison valid, the receiver may advertise at most 127 header credits and 2047 data credits (about {capKiB} KiB).",
  "ch4.p4": "By default, a 256-byte packet occupies the link for {fcTau} ns on Gen5 ×16. If the round trip is 1000 ns, keeping the link full needs {fcNeed} data credits in flight (about {fcNeedKB} KB). But the limit is 2047, which covers only {fcW} packets in flight at once. The result is that the link uses only {fcU}, which is {fcX} GB/s. The 1000 ns round trip is a <strong>tuned parameter</strong>; this page found no reliable measured value. Halve it to 500 ns and the link reaches {fcHalf}; double it to 2000 ns and only {fcDbl} is left. With 128-byte packets the limit is the header credits: 127 headers cover only {fc128W} packets in flight, so utilisation is {fc128}.",
  "ch4.p5": "From PCIe 4.0 the two ends can agree on <strong>scaled flow control</strong>: the counters stay the same width, but each credit can stand for 1, 4 or 16 units, so a much larger buffer can be advertised. At 16 times, that is up to {hdrS} header credits and {capS} data credits (about {capSKiB} KiB). Tick the box and the model uses the 16-times header limit and lets the data credits go up to 8192, so you can see how large a buffer would have to be. Credits are not unique to PCIe; Fibre Channel does the same. See <a href='../storage-over-a-network/#ch4'>“Drives Across a Wire”, Chapter 4</a>.",
  "ch4.limit": "The model has only one class of packet (writes) flowing in one direction, and the buffer size and round trip are assumed. The scaling factors (1, 4, 16) and their start in PCIe 4.0 come from the patent that proposed them (FPGA vendors’ guides only confirm the 127/2047 limits and that the scale fields exist); this page has not read the specification text.",
  "ch4.model.title": "Do the credits cover the round trip?",
  "ch4.model.how": "Set <strong>data per packet</strong>, the <strong>data credits advertised</strong> by the receiver and the <strong>round-trip time</strong>, and watch how the sender’s credits run down and come back.",
  "ch4.model.aria": "Line: the sender’s data credits in hand over time; each square along the bottom is one packet sent",
