  "ch5.title": "Resend when it goes wrong",
  "ch5.law": "Every TLP carries a 12-bit sequence number and a 32-bit check (LCRC). The receiver accepts packets in order and answers ACK if they are good, or NAK if they are bad. The sender keeps every unacknowledged packet in a buffer; on a NAK it resends everything from the bad one onwards.",
  "ch5.p1": "Bits on the wire do go wrong. The specification requires a bit error rate of no more than 10⁻¹² per lane. On Gen5 ×16 that still means about {e12} bits in error every second. That is not many, but it cannot be ignored. The data link layer finds them and makes them good, so nothing above it needs to know.",
  "ch5.p2": "Figure 7 shows one error. TLP 8’s LCRC fails, so the receiver discards it. TLP 9, right after it, is undamaged, but its sequence number is not the 8 the receiver is waiting for, so it is discarded too. The receiver answers with a NAK meaning “I have 7 and everything before it.” The sender resends from 8, replaying the packets in its buffer. This scheme is called <strong>go-back-N</strong>. The receiver never has to store out-of-order packets, but one error means resending a whole run.",
  "ch5.p3": "An ACK need not follow every packet. The receiver can collect a batch and confirm everything up to a sequence number with one ACK. If replies stop coming, the sender’s replay timer fires and it resends on its own. If three replays of the same packet make no progress, the 2-bit counter REPLAY_NUM rolls over to 0 on the fourth, and the link retrains before replaying.",
  "ch5.p4": "At 10⁻¹², the chance that a 256-byte packet is in error is only {p12}, so replays cost almost nothing. Push the rate to 10⁻⁸ and each packet has a {p8} chance of error, and efficiency is still {u8}. At 10⁻⁶ the chance per packet is {p6}, which looks small. But with go-back-N, each error means resending all the roughly {ratio} packets in flight, and efficiency drops to {u6}. Resending only the bad one would give {u6sr}. The replay buffer has to hold everything sent in one round trip, about {buf} KB.",
  "ch5.p5": "The 1000 ns round trip is a <strong>tuned parameter</strong>: halve it to 500 ns and efficiency at 10⁻⁶ is {u6half}; double it to 2000 ns and it is {u6dbl}. Changing the packet size does not rescue it either: at 4096 bytes it is {u6big}, at 128 bytes {u6small}. Big packets are more likely to be hit, but fewer are in flight, and the two nearly cancel. The sixth generation can accept a raw error rate of 10⁻⁶ only because it first corrects most errors on the link (Chapter 10). The same trade-off in RDMA networks is covered in <a href='../storage-over-a-network/#ch6'>“Drives Across a Wire”, Chapter 6</a>.",
  "ch5.limit": "The model assumes bit errors are independent and that the window covers one round trip. It leaves out the loss of a NAK itself, replay-timer timeouts, and the 16-bit CRC on the small ACK/NAK packets. The exact length of the replay timer has not been checked against the specification.",
  "ch5.model.title": "How much bandwidth errors eat",
  "ch5.model.how": "Drag the <strong>bit error rate</strong>, choose <strong>data per packet</strong> and the <strong>resend method</strong>, and watch the effective bandwidth fall.",
