  "ch6.title": "Roll call at boot",
  "ch6.law": "At boot, nobody knows what is hanging off the tree. The firmware or operating system starts at bus 0 and asks about each one in turn, numbers every bus, finds out how much address space each device needs, and then hands out address windows from the bottom up.",
  "ch6.p1": "Every PCIe function has a <strong>configuration space</strong>. Its first 64 bytes are a fixed-format header (Figure 8), holding the vendor ID, device ID, class code and up to six <strong>BARs</strong> (base address registers). The whole space is 4096 bytes. The first 256 bytes are the same as in old PCI, and the rest holds the extra capabilities PCIe added. Machines today access it through ECAM: the bus, device, function and register numbers are combined into one memory address, which is read or written directly.",
  "ch6.p2": "The roll call is depth-first. Starting at bus 0, it reads the vendor ID of each device number in turn; all 1s means nothing is there. When it finds a bridge (a root port, or a switch’s upstream or downstream port), it gives the bus on the far side a new number (the secondary bus), goes down and keeps counting. On the way back it writes the highest number it covers (the subordinate bus). This way every bridge knows which range of bus numbers is its own, so configuration requests can be routed down the right branch.",
  "ch6.p3": "A device states how much address space it needs through a small trick: write all 1s to its BAR and read it back. The device holds at 0 the low bits it does not use. Strip the flag bits from the read-back value, invert and add one, and you have the size. A BAR’s size is always a power of 2, and its address must be aligned to that size. Then, from the bottom up, each bridge needs one window that covers everything below it. Memory windows are sized in units of 1 MiB.",
  "ch6.p4": "On this example machine (the device list and the starting addresses are <strong>tuned parameters</strong>, chosen to show the rules; changing the starting address does not change any of the sizes below), the roll call finds {buses} buses. The devices’ BARs add up to {barSum} MiB, but the windows take {used} MiB, and alignment and the 1 MiB granularity waste {waste} MiB. Insert a card needing only {cardBar} KiB in the empty slot, and its downstream port takes a window of its own, {cardWin} MiB. With the slot empty, the total is {used0} MiB. With 64-bit addresses this waste does not matter, but in the range below 4 GiB it really can crowd out other things.",
  "ch6.p5": "After the roll call, each device’s BAR is a window in the memory address space. The CPU reads and writes that range, and the root complex and the bridges along the way route each request to the device by its window. The driver controls the device through this window (Chapter 7).",
  "ch6.limit": "This does not show prefetchable windows, 64-bit BARs, I/O space, virtual functions or the space reserved for hot-plug. Real firmware may allocate in a different order. Where in the boot the firmware does this roll call is covered in <a href='../how-a-computer-boots/#ch6'>“How a Computer Boots”, Chapter 6</a>.",
  "ch6.model.title": "Roll call, then address assignment",
  "ch6.model.how": "Press <strong>next step</strong> to see the roll call in order, or <strong>finish all</strong> to see the finished address assignment. Change the <strong>BAR size</strong> of the card in the empty slot to see how the windows move.",
