  "ch7.title": "An interrupt is also a memory write",
  "ch7.law": "A device does not call the CPU over a dedicated wire. It writes its data into memory itself (DMA). When the work is done, it writes a fixed value to an agreed address. That write is the interrupt.",
  "ch7.p1": "This is how a driver gets a device to work (Figure 9). It first prepares the command and data buffers in memory, then writes once to a doorbell register in the device’s BAR. The device hears the doorbell, sends read requests to fetch the command, then sends read and write requests to move the data. All of these are TLPs the device starts itself. This is <strong>DMA</strong>, and the data does not pass through the CPU. How NVMe’s queues and doorbells talk is shown in <a href='../why-nvme-is-fast/#ch4'>“Why NVMe Is So Fast”, Chapter 4</a>.",
  "ch7.p2": "The address a device sees is not necessarily the real physical address. Modern machines have an <strong>IOMMU</strong> in the root complex. Like the CPU’s page tables, it translates the addresses a device uses into physical addresses, and it stops the device from touching memory that is not its own. When a virtual machine is given a device directly, the IOMMU is what keeps that device’s accesses out of the rest of memory.",
  "ch7.p3": "When the work is done, the device has to tell the CPU. Old PCI had four dedicated interrupt wires, shared between devices. PCIe does not use such wires; it uses <strong>MSI</strong> (message-signalled interrupts). The driver tells the device an address and a data value in advance, and when the device wants an interrupt, it writes that data to that address. On x86, the address falls in a range starting with FEE. The root complex sees it and passes it to the interrupt controller. The address says which core to send the interrupt to, and the data holds the interrupt vector. <strong>MSI-X</strong> gives each device a whole table, 16 bytes per entry, allowing many interrupts, each sent to a different core.",
  "ch7.p4": "The “one real machine” record comes from lspci output on a Linux machine: address 0xfee0300c, data 0x4171. Decoded with the Intel manual: the destination field is 0x03 with RH=1 and DM=1, which is logical destination mode, so 0x03 is not one core’s number but a bit mask selecting a group of cores; delivery is lowest priority (to whichever core in that group is running at the lowest priority), and the vector is 0x71. An interrupt travels the same path as an ordinary write and is the same kind of packet, and under PCIe’s ordering rules it cannot overtake the writes the device sent earlier. So when the CPU receives the interrupt, the data is already in memory.",
  "ch7.p5": "This also explains how an NVMe drive lets each CPU core receive its own completion notices: each queue has its own MSI-X interrupt, sent to the matching core. When interrupts come too often, drivers simply stop waiting for them and poll instead. See <a href='../why-nvme-is-fast/#ch7'>“Why NVMe Is So Fast”, Chapter 7</a>.",
  "ch7.limit": "This covers only the x86 MSI address format. Other platforms, such as ARM, use different interrupt-controller formats. It also does not explain how the old interrupt wires are emulated with messages in PCIe.",
  "ch7.model.title": "Building an MSI",
  "ch7.model.how": "Choose a <strong>target core</strong> and an <strong>interrupt vector</strong>, and see the MSI-X table entry and each byte of the write packet on the wire; or look at a record read from a real machine.",
