  "ch7.say.own": "The device writes 0x{d} to address 0x{a}. The leading FEE says “this is an interrupt”; the target core is {dest}; the vector in the data is {vec}, delivered as “{mode}”. To the CPU this is just an ordinary memory write that happens to land on the interrupt controller’s address.",
  "ch7.say.real": "This one is from a real machine: address 0x{a}, data 0x{d}. Decoded, the destination field is {dest}, RH={rh}, DM={dm} (DM=1 means logical mode: the field is a bit mask selecting a group of cores, not one core’s number), vector {vec}, delivered as “{mode}”.",

  "ch8.title": "Faster means shorter",
  "ch8.law": "The higher the frequency, the more signal a trace loses per inch. Each generation has a total loss budget for the whole path. The package, the slot and the add-in card take a fixed share first, and what is left is for the motherboard trace.",
  "ch8.p1": "A signal on a circuit board loses energy in two ways. Copper losses (skin effect and surface roughness) rise with the square root of frequency, and dielectric absorption in the board material rises linearly with frequency. The frequency that matters is the <strong>Nyquist frequency</strong>, half the symbol rate: Gen5’s 32 GT/s gives 16 GHz; Gen6 uses PAM4, but its symbol rate is unchanged, so it is still 16 GHz; Gen7 is 32 GHz. For how to read a loss-against-frequency curve, see <a href='../../../signal/systems/frequency-response/'>“Frequency Response & Filters”</a>.",
  "ch8.p2": "Each generation gives the whole chip-to-chip path a total loss budget: Gen5 at 16 GHz is 36 dB; Gen6 is 32 dB (PAM4 leaves a smaller eye, Chapter 9); Gen7 at 32 GHz is about 36 dB. The budget is first shared out to the CPU package, the slot and the add-in card (Figure 10). Using the test configuration of an instrument maker, these three together take {fix5} dB for Gen5, leaving only {left5} dB for the motherboard trace.",
  "ch8.p3": "Ordinary FR-4 board material loses about {a16} dB per inch at 16 GHz; low-loss material about {a16l} dB. Divide the dozen-odd dB left over by that, and you know the reach. Losses are not a dead end. The receiver’s equalisers (CTLE lifts the high frequencies; DFE subtracts the tail using bits it has already decided) win back part of the budget, and the budget is the limit once those are counted. When a link is too long, add a <strong>retimer</strong>. It fully recovers the bits and sends them out again, splitting the link into two segments, each with a complete budget of its own. A link may have at most two retimers.",
  "ch8.p4": "By this model, Gen5 on ordinary FR-4 reaches only about {r5f} inches; with mid-loss material, {r5m} inches; with low-loss material, {r5l} inches. Gen6’s budget is tighter: mid-loss material leaves {r6m} inches. Gen7 doubles the frequency: {r7m} inches with mid-loss material and {r7l} inches with low-loss. This is why server motherboards increasingly use low-loss material and retimers, and move part of the link out of the board and into cables.",
  "ch8.p5": "Two values are <strong>tuned parameters</strong>. The board’s dielectric constant is 4.35, chosen so that mid-loss material at 10 GHz matches the dielectric loss given by Hargin. Gen6 and Gen7 package and connector losses are assumed equal to Gen5’s. If Gen7’s two figures are halved, mid-loss material reaches {r7mHalf} inches; if doubled, only {r7mDbl} inches.",
  "ch8.limit": "A real design needs a full channel simulation, covering reflections, crosstalk, vias and loss that varies with temperature. This model only adds up insertion loss by length. With retimers, the model charges each segment the same fixed loss, which is conservative. The Gen7 budget comes from vendor test material published before the specification was released. Vendors split the Gen5 budget slightly differently: Astera Labs gives 9 dB to the package and 16 dB to the board, which puts mid-loss reach at {r5mAlt} inches instead of {r5m}.",
  "ch8.model.title": "Does one trace stay within budget?",
