  "ch9.title": "Two bits per symbol",
  "ch9.law": "PAM4 uses four levels, so each symbol carries two bits: the symbol rate stays the same and the bit rate doubles. The price is three eyes squeezed into the same swing, each only a third as tall, which costs {pen} dB of signal-to-noise ratio.",
  "ch9.p1": "By the sixth generation, doubling the symbol rate again would put the Nyquist frequency at 32 GHz, and the loss would rise sharply. A 10-inch mid-loss trace loses {loss16} dB at 16 GHz and {loss32} dB at 32 GHz, a gap of {lossGap} dB. PCIe 6.0 chose not to raise the frequency but to change the modulation. NRZ carries one bit per symbol on two levels; PAM4 uses four levels, so each symbol carries two bits (Figure 11).",
  "ch9.p2": "An <strong>eye diagram</strong> overlays many symbols. The open space in the middle is the “eye”, and the receiver decides levels at its centre. Noise smears the lines and closes the eye. For the same peak-to-peak swing, each PAM4 eye is only a third the height of an NRZ eye, and under the same noise the error rate is worse by several orders of magnitude. Adjacent levels are assigned by Gray code, so a one-level slip flips only one bit.",
  "ch9.p3": "For NRZ to reach 10⁻¹², the signal-to-noise ratio (defined here as swing divided by the standard deviation of the noise) needs about {nrz12} dB. PAM4 needs {pam12} dB, which is {gap12} dB more. PCIe 6.0’s answer is not to chase 10⁻¹²: it accepts a raw error rate of 10⁻⁶, where PAM4 needs only {pam6} dB, {gapUse} dB more than NRZ needs for 10⁻¹². The remaining errors are left to FEC (Chapter 10).",
  "ch9.p4": "At the default setting, NRZ is already far below 10⁻¹², but PAM4 is still above 10⁻⁶. The same lane needs several more dB once the modulation changes. Roughly, doubling the frequency costs {lossGap} dB of loss, while PAM4 at 10⁻⁶ needs only {gapUse} dB more, which is much cheaper. These few dB are recovered by stronger equalisation (pre-emphasis at the transmitter, decision feedback at the receiver) and by better board material.",
  "ch9.p5": "PAM4 was not invented by PCIe; high-speed Ethernet had used it for some time. PCIe adopted it later because it is more sensitive to latency: errors must be corrected quickly and blocks must be small. That is where the FLIT of the next chapter comes from.",
  "ch9.limit": "The noise here is white Gaussian noise. It leaves out intersymbol interference, jitter and equalisation. Real eye diagrams are usually closed by these.",
  "ch9.model.title": "The same noise, two eyes",
  "ch9.model.how": "Drag the <strong>signal-to-noise ratio</strong> and see how the NRZ and PAM4 eyes are smeared by the noise, and how much their error rates differ.",
  "ch9.model.aria": "Two eye diagrams: NRZ with two levels and PAM4 with four, both with the same noise added",
  "ch9.ctl.snr": "Signal-to-noise ratio",
  "ch9.read.nrz": "NRZ error rate",
  "ch9.read.pam": "PAM4 error rate",
  "ch9.read.needn": "NRZ needs for 10⁻¹²",
  "ch9.read.needp": "PAM4 needs for 10⁻⁶",
  "ch9.cv.nrz": "NRZ: 1 bit per symbol",
  "ch9.cv.pam": "PAM4: 2 bits per symbol",
  "ch9.say.noisy": "Too much noise: NRZ’s error rate is {n}, not yet at 10⁻¹²; PAM4’s is {p}.",
  "ch9.say.mid": "NRZ is enough ({n}), but PAM4 is not: {p}, above the 10⁻⁶ that FEC can handle.",
  "ch9.say.clean": "Both are enough: NRZ {n}; PAM4 {p}, already below 10⁻⁶, and the rest is left to FEC.",

  "ch10.title": "Fixed-size FLITs: correct first, then resend",
  "ch10.law": "From Gen6, the link no longer sends TLPs one at a time. It sends fixed 256-byte FLITs: 236 bytes carry TLPs, and the rest is link-layer information, CRC and FEC. FEC corrects most errors on the spot, the CRC catches what is left, and only then is anything resent.",
  "ch10.p1": "At a raw error rate of 10⁻⁶, the Chapter 5 method would give every packet a chance of error of a few in a thousand, and go-back-N would eat about half the bandwidth. So Gen6 adds <strong>forward error correction</strong> (FEC) before any resending. The sender adds a few check bytes, and the receiver uses them to correct the bad bytes directly, without waiting for a resend. The basic idea of error-correcting codes is shown by the Hamming code in <a href='../../../tech/devices/inside-a-phone/#ecc'>“Ten Ideas Inside One Phone”</a>.",
  "ch10.p2": "FEC needs fixed-size blocks, so Gen6 cuts the link’s data into 256-byte <strong>FLITs</strong> (Figure 12): 236 bytes of TLP, 6 bytes of data-link information (acknowledgements and credits), 8 bytes of CRC and 6 bytes of FEC. The FEC is interleaved across three paths, each able to correct one bad byte. PAM4 errors often come in runs, and interleaving spreads a run across the three paths. After correction, the CRC checks again, and a FLIT that still fails is resent.",
  "ch10.p3": "Error checking and correction together add less than {decNs} ns of latency. TLPs no longer carry their own sequence numbers, LCRCs and framing: those moved into the FLIT and are computed once per FLIT. So although the FLIT has a fixed overhead of {flitOh}, it saves the 8 bytes each packet used to carry.",
  "ch10.p4": "At 10⁻⁶ with no FEC, each FLIT has a {pNo} chance of needing a resend. The roughly {flitRatio} FLITs in flight in a round trip would all have to be resent, leaving {uNo} of the bandwidth usable. Resending only the bad one still gives {uNoSr}. With FEC, the resend chance drops to {pFec}, and usable bandwidth is {uFec}. The 1000 ns round trip is still a <strong>tuned parameter</strong>: without FEC, halving it gives {uNoHalf} and doubling gives {uNoDbl}; with FEC, almost nothing changes. Server drive interfaces also added FEC at 24G; see <a href='../how-sas-works/#ch9'>“Why Server Drives Use SAS”, Chapter 9</a>.",
  "ch10.p5": "FLITs are not always faster. A TLP waits on average half a FLIT before it can leave. Das Sharma’s 2022 presentation has a table that works this out in detail, and this model reproduces it. At ×1, a small packet with no data is {lat0x1} ns slower, while a 1024-DW packet is {lat1024x1} ns faster. On bandwidth, small packets gain the most: the per-packet overhead is spread out by the FLIT, so a 1-DW packet has {sc1} times Gen5’s bandwidth. Large packets gain less than double ({sc1024} times), because the FLIT’s fixed overhead is larger than the overhead large packets already had. The two curves cross at about {cross} DW ({crossB} bytes).",
  "ch10.p6": "So “Gen6 doubles the bandwidth” means more than double for small packets and slightly less than double for large ones. Gen7 keeps PAM4 and FLITs and only doubles the symbol rate. The Gen8 draft follows the same approach (Chapter 11).",
  "ch10.limit": "The model assumes byte errors are independent. Real PAM4 links produce errors in runs, which the specification handles with precoding and interleaving. CRC and FEC are counted by byte; the actual codes are not simulated.",
