  "ch11.title": "Where the frontier is (as of November 2026)",
  "ch11.law": "The items below fall into three groups: what has been settled in the specification, what has been built and tested, and what standards bodies or vendors say they will deliver. Sources and dates are in brackets.",
  "ch11.p1": "This page could not open the PCI-SIG website or its press releases directly. Every entry about PCI-SIG therefore comes second-hand, and is labelled as such.",
  "ch11.h.est": "Settled",
  "ch11.e1": "<strong>PCIe 7.0</strong> was released to members in June 2025: 128 GT/s, keeping PAM4 and FLITs, and {f7bi} GB/s combined for ×16 in both directions (raw rate, before FLIT overhead). The optical-aware retimer ECN published alongside it revised 6.4 and 7.0 so that retimers can connect to optical links. (reported by ServeTheHome, 2025-06-15; eeNews Europe, 2025-06-12)",
  "ch11.e2": "Draft 0.3 of <strong>PCIe 8.0</strong> went to members in September 2025, and draft 0.5 in May 2026, targeting {f8gts} GT/s. (reported by ServeTheHome, 2026-05-06, and Tom’s Hardware at the same time)",
  "ch11.e3": "<strong>CXL 4.0</strong> (November 2025) is built on the PCIe 7.0 physical layer, at 128 GT/s. (CXL Consortium white paper) How CXL carries memory and cache coherence over this link is covered in <a href='../coherent-interconnects/#ch3'>“How Chips Share Memory”, Chapter 3</a>.",
  "ch11.h.demo": "Built and tested",
  "ch11.d1": "The first PCIe 6.x compliance workshop ended on 31 July 2026. Credo’s Gen6 ×16 retimer passed compliance and joined the 6.x Integrators List. (Credo press release, reposted by innovationopenlab, 2026-08-31)",
  "ch11.d2": "Micron’s 9650 PCIe 6.0 SSD entered volume production in February 2026 (second-hand reports). Its speed and significance are covered in <a href='../why-nvme-is-fast/#ch8'>“Why NVMe Is So Fast”, Chapter 8</a>, and are not repeated here.",
  "ch11.d3": "Before that, the same Credo retimer, which can run PCIe 6.0, had already passed PCI-SIG compliance testing at 32 GT/s (Gen5 speed). (Credo press release, 2026-02-10)",
  "ch11.h.claim": "Announced plans",
  "ch11.c1": "The full PCIe 8.0 specification is targeted for 2028, at about {f8bi} GB/s combined for ×16 in both directions (raw rate). This is a plan, not a fact. (PCI-SIG, reported by ServeTheHome, 2026-05-06)",
  "ch11.c2": "Cadence calls the July 2026 event the “first” 6.x compliance workshop. This page found no PCI-SIG original to confirm that.",
  "ch11.c3": "The Gen7 loss budget in Chapter 8 (about 36 dB at 32 GHz) comes from vendor test material (Tektronix, Samtec) published around 2023–2024, before the specification was released. This page has not checked the values in the final specification.",
  "ch11.p2": "These entries will go out of date. The mechanisms in Chapters 1 to 10 will not change with them.",
  "ch11.limit": "This page does not benchmark or recommend any product.",

  "ch12.title": "Back to the start",
  "ch12.law": "PCIe’s doubling over more than twenty years has come partly from raising the symbol rate, and partly from wasting less of each symbol. Every step has to be paid for physically.",
  "ch12.q1": "<strong>What generation, and how many lanes, did this link settle on?</strong> Bandwidth is the product of the two, and both ends use the highest level they share (Chapter 2).",
  "ch12.q2": "<strong>How big is each transfer?</strong> The smaller the packet, the larger the share taken by headers and completions. Credits and round trips decide whether the link can be filled (Chapters 3 and 4).",
  "ch12.q3": "<strong>What does this generation use to double?</strong> A higher frequency, a new encoding or a new modulation. Each step has a physical bill: loss, eye height and error correction (Chapters 8 to 10).",
  "ch12.sum": "Back to the two opening questions. Speed has doubled six times. Gen2, Gen4, Gen5 and Gen7 doubled the symbol rate; Gen3 replaced 8b/10b and cut the encoding overhead from {enc10} to {enc130}; Gen6 added PAM4 and FLITs. Each time, the cost was paid in board material, retimers, equalisation and error correction. On the wire, one read or write is one or more TLPs, with headers, sequence numbers and checks, sent when credits allow and then acknowledged or resent. The device moves its own data by DMA, and when it is done it calls the CPU with one memory write.",
  "ch12.links": "Further reading: <a href='../why-nvme-is-fast/'>“Why NVMe Is So Fast”</a> covers the most common storage protocol on PCIe. <a href='../how-usb-drives-talk/#ch8'>“Why a USB Drive Never Hits Its Label”, Chapter 8</a> covers how PCIe runs inside Thunderbolt and USB4 tunnels. <a href='../what-a-drive-is-made-of/#ch7'>“What a Drive Is Made Of”, Chapter 7</a> puts form factors, connectors, links and protocols on one map. <a href='../how-sata-works/'>“Why SATA Has Lasted Over Twenty Years”</a> covers the serial link that came before PCIe. <a href='../how-a-computer-is-wired/'>“How the Parts of a Computer Are Wired Together”</a> covers the shared bus before PCIe and where a motherboard’s lanes come from. <a href='../how-thunderbolt-works/'>“How Thunderbolt Works”</a> covers how the PCIe tunnel shares one USB-C cable with displays and USB 3.",

