  "footer": "<p><strong>Sources.</strong> D. Das Sharma, PCI Express 6.0 Specification (PCI-SIG / OFA presentation, 2022), the generation, latency and bandwidth tables on slides 4 and 13. R. Budruk, D. Anderson and T. Shanley, PCI Express System Architecture (MindShare), Chapter 3 (the publisher’s free sample chapter). D. Froelich, PCI Express 3.0 PHY Electrical Layer Requirements (PCI-SIG presentation, 2008), on the trade-off between 8 GT/s and 10 GT/s and on insertion loss. Linux kernel include/uapi/linux/pci_regs.h, include/linux/pci-ecam.h, drivers/pci/ecam.c and include/linux/pci.h (read 2026-11-15). AMD PCIe IP guide PG346 (the 7-bit Lower Address of completions). M. Ajanovic (Intel), PCIe 3.0 presentation at Hot Chips 2009 (the STP token). Astera Labs, how the PCIe 5.0 insertion-loss budget is split. NVIDIA patent US9904653 (scaled flow-control factors). Intel 64 and IA-32 Architectures Software Developer’s Manual, Volume 3A, §10.11 (MSI address and data format). E. Billauer’s blog, lspci output (an MSI example). G. Colton’s blog (2024-11-09) and Xillybus documentation: credit limits of 127 and 2047. W. Stallings, Data and Computer Communications, the ARQ section. J. Proakis, Digital Communications, M-PAM error rates. TI application note SNLA401 (loss budgets, retimers and redrivers); Tektronix’s 2023 PCIe 7.0 introduction and PCIe 6.0 test material; Samtec DesignCon 2024 presentation. Z. Hargin (Siemens, 2025-10-09), on conductor and dielectric loss. CXL Consortium, CXL 4.0 white paper. Credo press releases (2026-02-10, 2026-08-31). ServeTheHome reports (2025-06-15, 2026-05-06). The URL and access date for each source are given in BRIEF.md.</p><p>Written by an AI. The model is simplified, and the text marks which parameters were tuned. This is not a buying guide.</p>"
